/**
 * @addtogroup Foundation_BuildGuide_PSoC
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildPSoCTarget Initial Supported Target
 *
 * Initial PSoC target:
 *
 * @code
 * PSoC 5LP
 * ARM GCC
 * Cortex-M3
 * Soft Float
 * @endcode
 *
 * Target preset:
 *
 * @code
 * psoc5lp_armgcc_cortex_m3_soft
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildPSoCWhy Why not every chip
 *
 * Foundation should not initially generate one distribution per PSoC chip.
 *
 * The minimum useful split is:
 *
 * - toolchain,
 * - CPU core,
 * - ABI/FPU mode,
 * - configuration.
 *
 * Since Foundation is mostly portable C++ and does not depend on chip registers,
 * a PSoC 5LP Cortex-M3 soft-float build can cover the typical CY8CKIT-059 use case.
 * The preset produces a generic Cortex-M3 soft-float static archive. It does not
 * provide PSoC Creator startup code, generated components, linker script, device
 * headers, or project metadata.
 *
 * The consuming PSoC project must use a compatible Arm GNU compiler/runtime and
 * the same Thumb, Cortex-M3, and soft-float ABI. Compiler compatibility must be
 * validated with the exact PSoC Creator/toolchain version used by the product.
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildPSoCExport Export
 *
 * Example:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/export.ps1 psoc5lp_armgcc_cortex_m3_soft
 * @endcode
 *
 * or:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/export.sh psoc5lp_armgcc_cortex_m3_soft
 * @endcode
 *
 * Output:
 *
 * @code
 * dist/psoc5lp_armgcc_cortex_m3_soft/
 * @endcode
 *
 * Inspect both archives with `arm-none-eabi-objdump -f` and link them into a
 * minimal PSoC firmware before publishing. Do not use host CTest as proof of
 * embedded execution.
 */
